1. Nf3 c5 2. g3 g6 3. Bg2 Bg7 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nxd4 Nc6 6. Nb3 d6 7. c4 Be6 8. c5
Rc8 9. O-O Nf6 10. Nc3 O-O 11. cxd6 exd6 12. e4 Qd7 13. Bg5 h6 14. Bf4 Rfd8 15.
Qd2 g5 16. Be3 Ne5 17. Bd4 Bh3 18. f4 Nc4 19. Qe2 Bxg2 20. Kxg2 g4 21. Rae1 h5
22. Rh1 b5 23. h3 Re8 24. hxg4 hxg4 25. Rh4 {From this point on, neither
scoresheet is fully credible. White's is barely legible, while Black's is
reasonably legible in an unusual sort of descriptive notation but some of the
moves look wrong.} Nxe4 26. Bxg7 f5 27. Qd3 Qxg7 28. Qd5+ Kf8 29. Qxf5+ Nf6 30.
Rxe8+ Kxe8 ({Apparently leaving a rook en prise, which White doesn't take.
Instead Black would be fine after} 30... Rxe8 {. The score must be wrong but
no obvious way to fix it springs to mind. By now White's scoresheet had
descended into gibberish. It seems to give 29...Rxe8 but then White's 30th is
given as a blank then Black's 30th is Kxe8, then 31 Qd6ch Kd5 32 Ne4, the rest
cannot be read and scoring ceases completely after White's 35th. JS}) 31. Qe6+
{("QK6ch")} Kd8 32. Nd4 a6 {("R3" - Black recorded pawn moves thus - just the
target square in descriptive notation, e.g the first two moves of the game
were recorded "NKB3 QB4")} 33. Qe2 Qb7+ 34. Kg1 Qf7 35. Ne4 Kc7 ({Now Black
could win with} 35... Qd5) 36. Nxf6 Qxf6 {"and resigns".} 1-0

